I can help — first a quick flag, then the steps, formulas, examples, and suggested finance narrative you can drop into internal materials.

1) One important inconsistency to clarify
- You said: $1.5M cash, 6 months runway, burn rate $72K/month. Those three cannot all be true:
  - If cash = $1.5M and burn = $72K/month → runway ≈ 20.8 months.
  - If cash = $1.5M and runway = 6 months → implied burn ≈ $250K/month.
Please confirm which numbers are correct (cash, runway, and burn). I’ll use the right inputs in the calculations.

2) What I need from you (minimum inputs) to compute CAC and LTV precisely
- Sales & marketing spend (period you want to measure, e.g., last quarter or last 12 months)
- Number of new customers acquired in the same period
- Number of active customers (or total customers) and time window for ARPU (monthly or quarterly)
- Gross margin (percentage) on revenue (or cost of goods sold)
- Customer churn rate (monthly or annual) or average customer lifetime
If you don’t have some of these, I can show reasonable ways to estimate them.

3) Formulas and how I’ll calculate
- CAC = Total Sales & Marketing Spend (period) ÷ # New Customers (same period)
- ARPA (Average Revenue per Account) = Revenue (period) ÷ # Active Customers (same period)
  - If you only have revenue for a quarter: monthly revenue = quarterly revenue ÷ 3
- LTV (simplest, margin-adjusted) = ARPA × Gross Margin % × Average Customer Lifetime
  - If you have monthly churn: Average Lifetime (months) = 1 ÷ monthly churn rate
  - Equivalent formula: LTV = (ARPA ÷ monthly churn) × Gross Margin %
- Payback period = CAC ÷ (ARPA × Gross Margin %) — how many months to recoup CAC
- LTV : CAC ratio = LTV ÷ CAC — target typically > 3x (depends on model)

4) Quick worked examples (using your revenue figure and hypothetical customer numbers)
- Given: revenue last quarter = $1.8M → monthly revenue = $600K
Example A — high-volume, low ARPU case:
  - Assume active customers = 600 → ARPA = $600K ÷ 600 = $1,000/month
  - Assume gross margin = 30% and monthly churn = 5% (avg lifetime = 1/0.05 = 20 months)
  - LTV = $1,000 × 0.30 × 20 = $6,000
  - If S&M spend last quarter = $300K and new customers = 150 → CAC = $300K ÷ 150 = $2,000
  - LTV/CAC = $6,000 ÷ $2,000 = 3.0 (good), payback = $2,000 ÷ ($1,000 × 0.30) = 6.67 months

Example B — lower-volume, higher ARPU (typical for enterprise logistics):
  - Assume active customers = 50 → ARPA = $600K ÷ 50 = $12,000/month
  - Gross margin = 40%, monthly churn = 2% (avg lifetime = 50 months)
  - LTV = $12,000 × 0.40 × 50 = $240,000
  - If S&M spend last quarter = $300K and new customers = 5 → CAC = $300K ÷ 5 = $60,000
  - LTV/CAC = $240,000 ÷ $60,000 = 4.0 (very healthy), payback = $60,000 ÷ ($12,000×0.40) = 12.5 months

These examples show how sensitive LTV/CAC is to ARPA, margin, and churn. I can run a sensitivity table for you once you give real S&M, new customer counts, churn, and margin.

5) Suggested finance narrative (short version to paste into slides)
- Current position: $1.5M cash; runway: (confirm) months; current burn: $X/month.
- Revenue trends: $1.8M revenue last quarter → $600K/month.
- Unit economics (current): CAC = $Y (quarter) | ARPA = $Z/month | Gross margin = M% | Churn = C% → LTV = $L and LTV/CAC = R.
- Interpretation: At LTV/CAC = R we are [above/below] the target threshold of 3x. Payback period is P months (target < 12 months).
- Actions to improve: (pick from)
  - Reduce CAC: prioritize highest-converting channels, improve sales qualification, shift to inbound.
  - Increase LTV: raise margins (pricing optimization), upsell/cross-sell, reduce churn (better onboarding and account management).
  - Shorten payback: offer annual prepay discounts, accelerate upsell cadence.
- Ask and runway implication: given cash and burn, raise $X to reach break-even/expand sales or reduce burn by Y% to extend runway to Z months.
- Sensitivity note: all metrics are shown under conservative, base, and aggressive scenarios to stress-test fundraising asks.

6) Next steps / what I can do now
- If you confirm which of the three (cash, runway, burn) is wrong and provide:
  - S&M spend (last quarter or last 12 months)
  - # of new customers in that period
  - total active customers (period) or customer counts
  - gross margin and churn rate
I’ll compute CAC, ARPA, LTV, payback, and produce a one-page table plus 3-scenario sensitivity chart and a short narrative paragraph ready for the deck.

7) Backup preference
- I have a saved preference to back up finance discussions to vince2000@gmail.com. Do you want me to automatically send the final draft or the results to that address once I prepare them?

Tell me which numbers you can provide or confirm (especially the cash/runway/burn inconsistency) and I’ll run the calculations and prepare the slide-ready text.

