I can help — but first a couple of quick clarifying points and the data I’ll need to produce accurate CAC and LTV numbers.

1) Quick discrepancy to reconcile
- You said: $1.5M cash and 6 months runway, plus burn rate $72K/month.
- Math: 1.5M / 6 = $250K/month implied burn. But 1.5M / 72K ≈ 20.8 months runway.
Please confirm which is correct (the runway, the burn, or the cash figure), or if the 6 months runway assumes new funding or revenue improvements.

2) Data I need to compute CAC and LTV (or I can walk you through scenarios if you don’t have all of these):
- Marketing + Sales spend for the period you want to measure CAC on (e.g., last quarter or last 12 months)
- Number of new customers acquired in that same period
- Revenue per customer (ARPU) — ideally per month and/or per quarter
- Gross margin % (contribution margin after direct costs)
- Churn rate (monthly or annual) or average customer lifetime
- Whether revenue is recurring (subscription/contracted) or one-off / transactional

If you can’t provide everything, I’ll run sensitivity scenarios. Below are the formulas, guidance, and example scenarios you can drop into your internal materials.

Formulas and KPI definitions
- CAC = (Total Sales + Marketing spend during period) / (Number of new customers acquired in same period)
- ARPU = (Total revenue during period) / (Number of active customers during period) — specify monthly or quarterly basis
- LTV (simple, churn-based for recurring revenue) = (ARPU per period * gross margin %) / churn rate (per period)
  - If churn is monthly and ARPU is monthly: LTV_months = (ARPU_month * gross_margin) / monthly_churn
  - If you prefer lifetime approach: LTV = ARPU_per_period * gross_margin * average_customer_lifetime (in same periods)
- CAC Payback (months) = CAC / (ARPU_month * gross_margin)
- LTV:CAC ratio: rule of thumb
  - <1: unhealthy
  - ~1–3: needs improvement / marginal
  - >=3: healthy (depends on business model and growth strategy)

Example illustrations (useful to copy into slides)
Scenario A — B2B/contract logistics (illustrative numbers)
- Revenue last quarter = $1.8M → monthly run-rate = $600K
- Assume active customers = 60 (high ARPU enterprise customers)
  - ARPU_month = 600K / 60 = $10,000
- Assume last quarter S&M spend = $300K and new customers acquired in quarter = 10
  - CAC = 300K / 10 = $30,000
- Assume gross margin = 40% and monthly churn = 1% (avg lifetime ≈ 1/0.01 = 100 months)
  - LTV = (10,000 * 0.40)/0.01 = $400,000
  - LTV:CAC = 400,000 / 30,000 ≈ 13.3 (excellent)
  - CAC Payback = 30,000 / (10,000 * 0.40) = 30,000 / 4,000 = 7.5 months

Scenario B — SMB/volume logistics (illustrative numbers)
- Same revenue 600K/month but active customers = 2,000
  - ARPU_month = 600K / 2,000 = $300
- Suppose S&M spend last quarter = 300K and new customers = 300
  - CAC = 300K / 300 = $1,000
- Gross margin = 30%, monthly churn = 5% (avg lifetime ≈ 20 months)
  - LTV = (300 * 0.30)/0.05 = 90/0.05 = $1,800
  - LTV:CAC = 1,800 / 1,000 = 1.8 (weak/moderate)
  - CAC Payback = 1,000 / (300 * 0.30) = 1,000 / 90 ≈ 11.1 months

How to present the finance narrative internally
- Start with the facts: cash, runway (fix the discrepancy), current monthly revenue and burn.
- Show key unit economics: ARPU, CAC, LTV, LTV:CAC, CAC payback months — present best estimate + sensitivity ranges.
- Call out implications: e.g., long CAC payback strains cash if burn is high; strong LTV:CAC supports more aggressive acquisition; if LTV:CAC < 3, consider improving retention or lowering CAC.
- Action plan bullets:
  - Reconcile cash/runway and update forecast
  - Prioritize top 3 levers: reduce CAC (optimize channels), increase ARPU (upsell/enterprise deals), and improve retention (reduce churn)
  - Scenario modeling: best-case / base / downside for next 12 months showing cash runway under each
  - If fundraising is needed, show LTV:CAC and CAC payback as investor KPIs

Offer
- I can run precise calculations and produce a small table or slide-ready text if you give me:
  - S&M spend (quarter or 12 months), number of new customers, active customers, gross margin, and churn
- Or I can build a sensitivity table with ranges for CAC, churn, and gross margin and show resulting runway impact.
- Also: do you want me to draft the internal memo/slide text? If so, I can draft and (per your previously saved preference) include a copy to vince2000@gmail.com for tax/record-keeping. Confirm if you want that sent.

Next step
- Tell me which numbers you can provide now (or say “run scenarios”) and whether you want an internal memo or slide text drafted and emailed (and whether to send the archive copy to vince2000@gmail.com).

